1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,020
More than twelve thousand years ago,
in what is now Utah,

2
00:00:03,380 --> 00:00:06,980
someone sat by a fire
and left behind a few tobacco seeds.

3
00:00:07,776 --> 00:00:10,516
About eight thousand years ago,
in the Andes,

4
00:00:10,596 --> 00:00:14,016
people were chewing coca leaves
with a pinch of powdered lime.

5
00:00:14,377 --> 00:00:17,457
And about nine thousand years ago,
in a village in China,

6
00:00:17,637 --> 00:00:20,717
people were brewing a drink
from rice, honey and fruit.

7
00:00:21,343 --> 00:00:23,043
These people never met.

8
00:00:23,343 --> 00:00:26,903
They didn't share a language,
a religion, or even a continent.

9
00:00:27,413 --> 00:00:30,913
And yet each of them found a plant
that changes how a human brain works.

10
00:00:31,143 --> 00:00:33,913
And kept coming back to it.

11
00:00:34,423 --> 00:00:37,143
Nobody knows when humans first did this.

12
00:00:37,803 --> 00:00:39,683
And these are just three examples.

13
00:00:40,463 --> 00:00:42,783
Opium in the Bronze Age Mediterranean.

14
00:00:43,763 --> 00:00:46,203
Cannabis in the mountains of Central Asia.

15
00:00:47,298 --> 00:00:48,378
So why?

16
00:00:48,458 --> 00:00:53,938
Why does every human society, sooner or
later, find a way to change its own mind?

17
00:00:54,764 --> 00:00:57,424
And there's a stranger question
hiding underneath.

18
00:00:58,144 --> 00:01:02,044
Why does a plant contain a molecule
that fits a human brain at all?

19
00:01:04,454 --> 00:01:07,134
A coffee bush doesn't care
whether you're awake.

20
00:01:07,834 --> 00:01:10,174
A tobacco plant has never met a smoker.

21
00:01:10,995 --> 00:01:13,495
The answer is that these molecules
weren't made for us.

22
00:01:13,725 --> 00:01:15,535
They're weapons.

23
00:01:16,805 --> 00:01:19,885
Plants can't run away
from the animals that eat them.

24
00:01:20,585 --> 00:01:22,485
So many of them fight with chemistry.

25
00:01:23,388 --> 00:01:26,108
In 1984, a researcher in Boston

26
00:01:26,338 --> 00:01:30,188
mixed ground coffee and tea leaves
into the food of caterpillars.

27
00:01:30,716 --> 00:01:33,376
They ate less, trembled,
and stopped growing.

28
00:01:33,716 --> 00:01:36,516
At higher doses,
they were dead within a day.

29
00:01:37,129 --> 00:01:40,449
Nicotine is so good at this
that farmers sprayed tobacco water

30
00:01:40,679 --> 00:01:42,369
on their crops for centuries.

31
00:01:43,269 --> 00:01:47,569
And some of today's most widely used
insecticides are modelled on it.

32
00:01:48,107 --> 00:01:51,087
Even cocaine seems to protect
the coca plant.

33
00:01:51,727 --> 00:01:53,787
At the levels found in its leaves,

34
00:01:53,987 --> 00:01:56,867
it scrambles the nerves
of the insects that chew them.

35
00:01:57,357 --> 00:01:59,757
And here's why the weapon works on us too.

36
00:02:00,577 --> 00:02:04,537
An insect's nervous system and yours
run on many of the same chemicals.

37
00:02:05,348 --> 00:02:08,568
Dopamine. Serotonin. Acetylcholine.

38
00:02:08,948 --> 00:02:10,628
Insects use them too.

39
00:02:11,208 --> 00:02:15,268
Some of these signalling systems have been
around for hundreds of millions of years.

40
00:02:15,644 --> 00:02:17,144
So when a plant evolves a molecule

41
00:02:17,374 --> 00:02:21,724
that jams an insect's nerves,
it often jams ours as well.

42
00:02:22,289 --> 00:02:26,089
The same molecule can have very
different effects in different animals.

43
00:02:26,769 --> 00:02:29,549
Dose matters. So does biology.

44
00:02:30,496 --> 00:02:32,956
The caffeine in your coffee
is the same defensive chemical

45
00:02:33,186 --> 00:02:34,856
that helps protect the plant.

46
00:02:34,936 --> 00:02:37,496
Just in a very different dose.

47
00:02:40,216 --> 00:02:43,356
And plants don't only use
these chemicals as weapons.

48
00:02:43,952 --> 00:02:47,232
Coffee and citrus flowers
put a little caffeine in their nectar.

49
00:02:47,912 --> 00:02:49,512
Not enough to harm a bee.

50
00:02:49,972 --> 00:02:51,772
Just enough to change it.

51
00:02:52,351 --> 00:02:55,551
In 2013, researchers found
that bees given caffeine

52
00:02:55,781 --> 00:03:00,151
were three times as likely
to remember a flower's scent a day later.

53
00:03:00,724 --> 00:03:02,104
Plants were already using chemistry

54
00:03:02,334 --> 00:03:05,624
to influence their visitors
long before we came along.

55
00:03:08,264 --> 00:03:11,344
But that only explains
why these molecules work.

56
00:03:12,024 --> 00:03:14,284
It doesn't explain why we want them.

57
00:03:14,847 --> 00:03:17,107
A poison should taste like a warning.

58
00:03:17,707 --> 00:03:19,887
So why would a brain go looking for it?

59
00:03:22,107 --> 00:03:25,547
To answer that,
scientists had to look inside the brain.

60
00:03:26,347 --> 00:03:29,987
And for a long time, the best map they had
was the drugs themselves.

61
00:03:30,973 --> 00:03:34,333
In 1973, three laboratories,
working independently,

62
00:03:34,563 --> 00:03:38,093
found receptors in the brain
that morphine locks onto.

63
00:03:38,712 --> 00:03:40,932
Which raised an obvious question.

64
00:03:41,572 --> 00:03:44,032
Why would a human brain
have a lock for a poppy?

65
00:03:44,826 --> 00:03:47,946
Two years later,
researchers in Aberdeen found the key.

66
00:03:48,626 --> 00:03:50,526
The brain makes its own opioids.

67
00:03:51,388 --> 00:03:53,288
We now call them endorphins.

68
00:03:53,748 --> 00:03:55,728
The name means "the morphine within".

69
00:03:57,057 --> 00:03:59,297
Cannabis led to the same discovery.

70
00:03:59,997 --> 00:04:05,917
First a receptor its active compound fits,
then, in 1992, the brain's own version.

71
00:04:06,605 --> 00:04:11,145
Its discoverers named it anandamide,
after the Sanskrit word for bliss.

72
00:04:11,643 --> 00:04:14,383
And nicotine gave its name
to a whole family of receptors,

73
00:04:14,613 --> 00:04:17,443
in your muscles as well as your brain.

74
00:04:18,517 --> 00:04:21,797
They don't need to invent
a new system inside your brain.

75
00:04:22,057 --> 00:04:24,917
They hijack systems
that were already there.

76
00:04:25,485 --> 00:04:28,845
Caffeine blocks a signal
that builds up the longer you're awake,

77
00:04:29,045 --> 00:04:30,925
the one that makes you feel tired.

78
00:04:31,575 --> 00:04:35,495
Nicotine imitates a messenger
the brain uses for attention.

79
00:04:36,001 --> 00:04:39,181
And cocaine stops the brain
from clearing away dopamine,

80
00:04:39,401 --> 00:04:41,821
so it keeps working
for longer than it should.

81
00:04:42,411 --> 00:04:45,071
Dopamine is often called
the pleasure chemical.

82
00:04:45,771 --> 00:04:47,011
It isn't, really.

83
00:04:47,715 --> 00:04:49,495
It's closer to a teacher.

84
00:04:50,195 --> 00:04:53,795
Among other things, it helps the brain
learn what's worth doing again.

85
00:04:54,126 --> 00:04:57,346
So a substance that pushes on it
doesn't just feel good.

86
00:04:57,886 --> 00:05:01,186
It can teach the brain
to treat it as something worth repeating.

87
00:05:03,836 --> 00:05:06,616
Then why didn't evolution
protect us from that?

88
00:05:07,060 --> 00:05:10,040
With one substance,
it seems to have done the opposite.

89
00:05:10,889 --> 00:05:14,369
About ten million years ago,
our ape ancestors picked up

90
00:05:14,599 --> 00:05:17,669
a single mutation
in a gene that breaks down alcohol.

91
00:05:18,345 --> 00:05:21,725
It made them about forty times better
at breaking it down.

92
00:05:22,345 --> 00:05:24,245
One explanation is fruit.

93
00:05:25,025 --> 00:05:27,965
Around that time, our ancestors
were spending more time on the ground,

94
00:05:28,195 --> 00:05:30,865
where ripe fruit falls and ferments.

95
00:05:31,553 --> 00:05:34,933
Our ancestors may have gained
an advantage from eating fermented fruit,

96
00:05:35,163 --> 00:05:38,353
millions of years
before anyone brewed a drop.

97
00:05:41,003 --> 00:05:44,323
And not everyone who tries a drug
gets caught by it.

98
00:05:44,853 --> 00:05:48,573
In the early 1990s,
a large American survey estimated

99
00:05:48,803 --> 00:05:53,373
how many people who had tried a substance
went on to become dependent on it.

100
00:05:53,909 --> 00:05:55,749
For cannabis, about nine per cent.

101
00:05:55,979 --> 00:05:59,429
For alcohol, about fifteen.
For heroin, twenty-three.

102
00:06:00,814 --> 00:06:03,414
And the highest of all
wasn't heroin or cocaine.

103
00:06:04,074 --> 00:06:06,694
It was tobacco. Nearly a third.

104
00:06:07,378 --> 00:06:10,458
So the risk depends on the substance,
and on the person.

105
00:06:11,258 --> 00:06:15,678
Genes, mental health, stress, and how
often it's used all change the odds.

106
00:06:18,318 --> 00:06:20,998
And chemistry still isn't the whole story.

107
00:06:21,393 --> 00:06:24,133
The most widely used drug on Earth
is caffeine.

108
00:06:24,973 --> 00:06:27,473
And hardly anyone drinks coffee
for the thrill.

109
00:06:28,125 --> 00:06:30,105
They drink it to get through a morning.

110
00:06:30,405 --> 00:06:33,265
To stay awake on a night shift.
To feel normal.

111
00:06:33,877 --> 00:06:35,777
That's what a lot of drug use is.

112
00:06:36,357 --> 00:06:39,637
Not chasing a high,
but getting away from something. Pain.

113
00:06:39,867 --> 00:06:42,657
Fear. Exhaustion.

114
00:06:43,097 --> 00:06:44,917
A memory you'd rather not have.

115
00:06:46,877 --> 00:06:50,197
Some cultures used psychedelic plants
in ceremonies,

116
00:06:50,377 --> 00:06:53,637
where an altered state
meant contact with something sacred.

117
00:06:54,290 --> 00:06:58,870
Others used alcohol to mark a wedding,
or to turn strangers into friends.

118
00:06:59,477 --> 00:07:04,477
And which of these substances a society
accepts isn't decided by chemistry at all.

119
00:07:06,267 --> 00:07:08,427
By the fifteenth century,
coffee was spreading

120
00:07:08,657 --> 00:07:10,847
through Sufi communities in Yemen,

121
00:07:10,947 --> 00:07:13,387
where it kept people awake
for night prayers.

122
00:07:14,149 --> 00:07:17,129
In 1511, the governor of Mecca banned it.

123
00:07:17,629 --> 00:07:19,029
The ban didn't last.

124
00:07:19,794 --> 00:07:25,794
In 1675, King Charles the Second tried to
shut down every coffee house in England.

125
00:07:26,594 --> 00:07:29,514
He backed down
before the order even took effect.

126
00:07:29,981 --> 00:07:33,541
And in the seventeenth century,
the Pope threatened to excommunicate

127
00:07:33,771 --> 00:07:36,741
anyone who used tobacco
in the churches of Seville.

128
00:07:37,373 --> 00:07:39,193
Today, coffee is breakfast.

129
00:07:39,933 --> 00:07:41,653
And tobacco, which, in that survey,

130
00:07:41,883 --> 00:07:45,693
hooked more of its users than heroin,
is sold at the petrol station.

131
00:07:48,423 --> 00:07:51,163
For most of history,
all of this came straight from plants.

132
00:07:51,243 --> 00:07:55,483
You chewed the leaf. You drank the brew.
You smoked the dried flower.

133
00:07:56,607 --> 00:08:00,207
Then, in the early 1800s,
a young German pharmacist

134
00:08:00,437 --> 00:08:02,727
pulled a single compound out of opium.

135
00:08:03,509 --> 00:08:07,949
He named it after Morpheus,
the god of dreams. Morphine.

136
00:08:08,535 --> 00:08:10,915
For the first time,
one of opium's active compounds

137
00:08:11,145 --> 00:08:14,415
could be isolated, purified and measured.

138
00:08:14,816 --> 00:08:18,076
That changed medicine.
And it changed the risk.

139
00:08:19,546 --> 00:08:23,766
Around 1860, a German chemist
did the same with coca.

140
00:08:24,106 --> 00:08:27,666
A coca leaf is typically
less than one per cent cocaine.

141
00:08:27,846 --> 00:08:31,406
Purified, it's a hundred times
more concentrated.

142
00:08:32,233 --> 00:08:38,433
And in August 1897, a chemist at Bayer
prepared two compounds, eleven days apart.

143
00:08:39,027 --> 00:08:40,487
One became aspirin.

144
00:08:40,567 --> 00:08:42,627
The other became heroin.

145
00:08:43,392 --> 00:08:46,592
Heroin had actually been made
more than twenty years earlier.

146
00:08:47,252 --> 00:08:50,652
But Bayer turned it into a medicine,
and sold it for coughs.

147
00:08:52,852 --> 00:08:55,832
And increasingly,
chemists went beyond extraction.

148
00:08:56,772 --> 00:09:01,712
They began modifying molecules, and
creating compounds no plant had ever made.

149
00:09:02,315 --> 00:09:05,635
Some became essential medicines.
Modern anaesthetics.

150
00:09:05,865 --> 00:09:07,395
Antidepressants.

151
00:09:08,075 --> 00:09:10,195
Painkillers far stronger than morphine.

152
00:09:10,970 --> 00:09:13,610
Being new doesn't make a molecule
more dangerous.

153
00:09:14,430 --> 00:09:18,090
What matters is what it does,
how much of it you take, and how.

154
00:09:19,284 --> 00:09:20,924
But it did open a loophole.

155
00:09:21,724 --> 00:09:24,284
Laws usually ban specific molecules.

156
00:09:25,064 --> 00:09:27,084
Change one small piece of a banned one,

157
00:09:27,164 --> 00:09:30,864
and you may get something similar
that, legally, doesn't exist yet.

158
00:09:31,501 --> 00:09:35,221
At the end of 2024,
the European Union's drugs agency

159
00:09:35,451 --> 00:09:38,861
was monitoring more than a thousand
new psychoactive substances.

160
00:09:39,091 --> 00:09:44,041
Forty-seven of them were reported
for the first time that year.

161
00:09:44,213 --> 00:09:46,553
When one is banned, another can appear.

162
00:09:47,013 --> 00:09:49,573
Faster than anyone can study what it does.

163
00:09:52,053 --> 00:09:54,453
And now the question is turning around.

164
00:09:55,113 --> 00:09:58,093
It's no longer just
what nature happens to contain.

165
00:09:58,173 --> 00:10:00,973
It's what we want our brains to do.

166
00:10:01,518 --> 00:10:05,478
Since 2023, specially authorised
psychiatrists in Australia

167
00:10:05,708 --> 00:10:08,398
have been allowed to prescribe psilocybin,

168
00:10:08,478 --> 00:10:12,958
the compound in magic mushrooms,
for depression that nothing else helped.

169
00:10:13,603 --> 00:10:18,223
Millions of people take pills to sleep,
to focus, or to feel less afraid.

170
00:10:18,883 --> 00:10:22,463
The same molecule can be a medicine
when it's used to treat a patient,

171
00:10:22,743 --> 00:10:26,023
or an illegal drug
when it's possessed or sold unlawfully.

172
00:10:27,230 --> 00:10:28,650
The line between a drug, a medicine

173
00:10:28,880 --> 00:10:31,550
and a morning coffee
isn't written in the molecule.

174
00:10:32,070 --> 00:10:35,130
We draw it. And we keep moving it.

175
00:10:37,770 --> 00:10:40,410
So, why do humans keep inventing drugs?

176
00:10:41,594 --> 00:10:43,054
Partly, we don't.

177
00:10:43,514 --> 00:10:47,354
The first ones were invented by plants,
as weapons against insects.

178
00:10:48,199 --> 00:10:52,119
They work on us because our brains
are built from the same ancient parts.

179
00:10:52,460 --> 00:10:54,800
And they pull us back
because they push on the system

180
00:10:55,030 --> 00:10:57,180
that decides what's worth repeating.

181
00:10:57,953 --> 00:11:01,713
Everything else, the coffee houses,
the ceremonies, the laboratories,

182
00:11:01,873 --> 00:11:05,313
is what a curious species does
once it notices that.

183
00:11:07,353 --> 00:11:09,313
We didn't just invent drugs.

184
00:11:10,013 --> 00:11:11,373
We found out that the world is full

185
00:11:11,603 --> 00:11:14,753
of chemicals that can change
what it feels like to be human.

186
00:11:15,045 --> 00:11:18,445
And once you've found that out,
it's very hard to stop looking.

187
00:11:20,355 --> 00:11:22,135
So here's a question for you.

188
00:11:22,835 --> 00:11:25,235
If a pill could make you sharper,
calmer or braver,

189
00:11:25,465 --> 00:11:28,535
with no side effects at all,
would that still be a drug?

190
00:11:29,295 --> 00:11:30,545
Let me know in the comments.
